  "notes": "West leads the ♣K against 4♥. The defenders cash 2 club tricks and switch to spades. South's problem is to avoid the loss of 2 diamond tricks. If diamonds are 3-3 there will be no problem.\n\nIf diamonds are 4-2, South can win the spade shift, cash the ♦A and ♦K and concede a diamond trick before drawing trump. The ♥Q can later be used to ruff South's remaining diamond loser. If East has 5 diamonds, the contract is likely to fail. However, if West has 5 diamonds, South can succeed with careful play as long as East has less than 4 hearts.\n\nSouth should start by winning the spade shift in the closed hand. South cashes 1 top diamond honour and crosses to the ♠A. A diamond is next played from North. If East ruffs, he is ruffing his partner's trick. South will later be able to draw 2 rounds of trumps and ruff his diamond loser in the dummy.\n\nIf East discards, South wins and concedes a diamond trick to West. The ♥Q can later be used to ruff South's remaining diamond loser.",
